You might take a look at svk, a layer on top of SVN that makes
branching and merging a lot easier (as well as allowing you to
make local repository branches and do work on them in useful ways):
http://svk.bestpractical.com/view/HomePage
Unforunately the documentation isn't great yet, but we're working on it
(I'm not a developer, but I'm involved in improving the documentation,
and I use it for most of my personal projects).  

svk also lets you get a local repository set up _really_ quickly if that's
all you want to do, which is why I find it great for little projects. If
you then decide you want to push your project up to the public, you can
trivially put it into public-facing SVN, since svk has an SVN backend.

For me, moving from CVS to SVN was simple; SVN gives you the svn mv 
command, which does the right things, while in CVS if you don't pick
your directory structure right the first time, you are Sad. Other version
control systems also merit consideration --- I know some nethack people
who swear by darcs --- but I'm very happy with SVN and svk.

